{
  "id": "ep005",
  "duration": 51.361,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.15,
      "end": 3.599,
      "text": "A black hole replacing our Sun would not suck Earth in.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Black hole - Messier 87 crop max res.png",
        "credit": "Event Horizon Telescope",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 3.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.599,
      "end": 11.244,
      "text": "Hollywood often portrays these compact objects as cosmic vacuum cleaners devouring everything in their surroundings.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Black Hole Desktop & Phone Wallpapers (SVS14146 - BlackHoleDisk desktop).png",
        "credit": "NASA's Scientific Visualization Studio - KBR Wyle Services, LLC/Scott Wiessinger, University of Maryland College Park/Barb Mattson, University of Maryland College Park/Francis Reddy, University of Mar",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Hole_Desktop_%26_Phone_Wallpapers_(SVS14146_-_BlackHoleDisk_desktop).png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.6,
          "end": 11.233,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.244,
      "end": 19.464,
      "text": "However, Albert Einstein's theory of general relativity describes gravity as the curvature of spacetime caused by mass.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Einstein 1921 by F Schmutzer - restoration.jpg",
        "credit": "Ferdinand Schmutzer / Adam Cuerden",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einstein_1921_by_F_Schmutzer_-_restoration.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.233,
          "end": 15.333,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 15.333,
          "end": 19.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 19.464,
      "end": 26.259,
      "text": "Because the pull depends only on mass, replacing the Sun with an identical-mass black hole changes nothing.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The Sun by the Atmospheric Imaging Assembly of NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory - 20100819.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/SDO (AIA)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sun_by_the_Atmospheric_Imaging_Assembly_of_NASA%27s_Solar_Dynamics_Observatory_-_20100819.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 19.467,
          "end": 22.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 22.867,
          "end": 26.267,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 26.259,
      "end": 32.954,
      "text": "The solar material would simply compress into an event horizon boundary only three kilometers wide.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Anatomy of a Black Hole (eso1907h).tiff",
        "credit": "ESO",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatomy_of_a_Black_Hole_(eso1907h).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 26.267,
          "end": 29.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 29.6,
          "end": 32.967,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 32.954,
      "end": 38.924,
      "text": "To get trapped, an object must cross this Schwarzschild radius where even light cannot escape.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "First image of our black hole (with wider background) (eso2208-eht-mwh).tiff",
        "credit": "EHT Collaboration",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_image_of_our_black_hole_(with_wider_background)_(eso2208-eht-mwh).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 32.967,
          "end": 35.3,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 35.3,
          "end": 38.933,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 38.924,
      "end": 46.574,
      "text": "Outside that boundary, infalling matter actually heats up and emits X-rays before crossing the point of no return.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "'Death spiral' around Cygnus XR-1 (opo0103b).jpg",
        "credit": "Ann Feild (STScI)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Death_spiral%27_around_Cygnus_XR-1_(opo0103b).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 38.933,
          "end": 46.567,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 46.574,
      "end": 51.011,
      "text": "But at our distant orbit, Earth would safely circle the dark remnant forever.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Black hole - Messier 87 crop max res.png",
        "credit": "Event Horizon Telescope",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 46.567,
          "end": 51.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
